      "element_id": "prisma_2020_23a_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_13",
      "quote": "The data do not show a clear superiority or inferiority for any of the treatments evaluated. ... Cochrane reviews and the American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons both labeled the evidence as 'inconclusive' [124, 125]; [126]; [12]. This review focused on extra-articular distal radius fractures only, where both Cochrane and the AAOS stated this inconclusiveness almost a decade ago for intra-articular fractures as well. ... Diaz-Garcia et al. published a systematic review in 2011 to examine outcomes of unstable distal radius fractures after treatment with either volar locking plate, non-bridging external fixation, bridging external fixation, percutaneous Kirschner-wire fixation, or plaster cast immobilization. They also found that plaster casting was associated with worse radiological outcomes than operative treatment [128]. Our systematic review reveals that since 2011 nothing has really changed.",
      "reason": "The discussion explicitly compares the review's findings to prior evidence (Cochrane, AAOS, Diaz-Garcia et al.) and notes consistency with earlier inconclusive findings and prior systematic review conclusions, thereby interpreting results in the context of other evidence."
    }
